  "factRole": "A relay has outside power, such as USB, and listens all the time, passing on texts for the nodes around it: the more relays, the farther texts reach. A sleepy node is on battery: to last, it sleeps and wakes only in short windows, and sends and receives through one relay near it, its anchor. A fixed relay that loses its power keeps relaying on battery until the battery runs low, and then turns sleepy. Holding the node's button switches its role until its power next changes.",
  "@factRole": {
    "description": "The help under the row Role of a node's sheet on the map; the other fact messages are the help of the rows they name."
  },
  "factMoving": "The node tells this itself from its GPS: after a little over a minute of positions away from where it stood, it counts as carried, and after four hours in one place it stays put again. A carried node's place follows its GPS, and it keeps no new mail for others, as it may soon be gone. Setting its place in the app's Settings makes it stay put at once.",
  "factAnchor": "A sleepy node sends and receives through one relay near it, its anchor. The anchor keeps the sleepy node's mail and hands it over in the short window after each time it wakes, and the sleepy node's address is its anchor's place. A sleepy node takes the first relay it hears as its anchor; with none yet it holds and passes on texts itself, at a higher cost to the channel.",
  "factPlace": "Texts find a node by its place: an address is a position and the node's ID, and each relay passes a text on toward that position. The place comes from the node's GPS, unless a person set it in the app's Settings, which wins, as for a relay indoors with no view of the sky. A node with no place yet sends no heartbeat, so no text can find it until it has one.",
  "factPower": "USB means outside power, on which a node can relay all the time. On battery the figure is read from the cell's voltage, so it is rough; on this node's own sheet it is the raw reading in millivolts. At about a fifth left the battery counts as low, and a relay on it turns sleepy to save the rest.",
  "factLink": "How well this node hears the other one: how far its signal is above the radio noise here. Below about 4 dB a full text is often lost when the signal fades, so the link is fragile and used only when no other is left; from about 7 dB it is good; between, fair. It is measured one way: the other node may hear this one better or worse.",
  "factLastHeard": "Every node sends a short heartbeat now and then, saying who and where it is. A relay sends one every 5 to 15 seconds, each on the next of 13 channels, so this node hears it about once every one to three minutes; a sleepy node wakes to send one every two and a half to five minutes. A relay not heard for six and a half minutes, or a sleepy node for ten, leaves the list.",
  "factDistance": "The straight line from this node's place to the other's, worked out by the app from the two positions, so it is shown only when both have a place. How far radio reaches depends on height and on what stands between: relays on rooftops hear each other at about 3 km.",
  "factKnown": "What the node says of its own neighbours, in three counts. Heard: the nodes whose heartbeats it hears. Strong: of those, the ones heard well enough that a full text gets through a fade, so they likely hear it too. Exchanged: of those, the ones that have taken a text from it, which proves they hear it.",
  "factGpsSignal": "How strongly the node's GPS hears the satellites, in dB-Hz, as its last reading had it. Under {floor} the node takes no position from it; an open sky gives about 40. Move a node to where it reads higher, or set its place by hand.",
  "@factGpsSignal": {
    "placeholders": {"floor": {"type": "int", "description": "The weakest signal, in dB-Hz, the node takes a position from."}}
  },
  "factVersion": "The version of the firmware the node runs. A new version spreads by itself: a node given it, from a phone or over USB, passes it to the relays around it a piece at a time, leaving the channels to texts. A node takes only a newer version signed by the project, and while it gathers one this row says which and how many of its blocks it holds.",
  "factExchanged": "This node has handed it a text and it took it. That proves the link works both ways: it hears this node, as well as being heard.",
  "factDemoted": "It did not answer this node's last three tries to pass it a text, so it may no longer hear this node. Until it answers again, or for six and a half minutes, it is tried only after every other neighbour with a good enough link.",
  "factAvoided": "It turned a text down, asking for a wait, or did not answer one. Nothing is sent to it for a while; texts go round it meanwhile, and wait for it only where there is no other way.",
  "factPaired": "This phone is paired with this node: the node sends your texts into the network, and keeps the ones for you until the app reads them. Only the paired phone sees the node's neighbours on this map."
}
